      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In urban settings, news outlets tend to employ more negative and sensationalized language when describing \"riots\" compared to \"protests.\" This disparity is particularly pronounced in predominantly black or minority neighborhoods, where the term \"riot\" is often used to imply a sense of lawlessness and chaos, whereas \"protest\" is used in more affluent, white neighborhoods to convey a sense of peaceful, First Amendment-protected expression. For instance, a protest in a predominantly black neighborhood might be described as a \"riot\" with \"looting and arson,\" while a similar event in a white neighborhood would be referred to as a \"protest\" with \"clashes between police and demonstrators.\" This linguistic framing can perpetuate negative stereotypes and reinforce systemic racism.",
