      "content": "That's a very clear distinction in the wording. Looking at the two snippets, I noticed the suburban one focuses heavily on the \"coalition of business groups\" and \"community leaders,\" which gives it a very organized, institutional feel. In contrast, the inner-city one jumps straight to \"violence\" and \"chaos.\" \n\nTo round out my analysis, I'd like to look at the \"characterization of agency.\" In the suburban snippet, the participants are seen as \"advocating\" for something specific. In the second snippet, the participants are framed more as an unpredictable force. Do you think this difference in describing the *intent* of the participants—viewing one group as political actors and the other as a volatile crowd—is a consistent pattern in mainstream reporting, or is it more about the specific way certain neighborhoods are characterized?",
